---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

/**
 * Diagnostics, not a report card.
 *
 * Every panel is here because it answers a question that changes what you do:
 * rewrite a card, change the retention setting, or nothing. There is no total,
 * no grade and no trend arrow — a number you want to see go up is a number you
 * will eventually push up by rating dishonestly, and the ratings are the data.
 */
const decks = await getCollection('flashcards');
const cards = decks.flatMap((deck) =>
  deck.data.cards.map((card) => ({ ...card, topic: deck.data.topic, deck: deck.data.deck }))
);
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base title="Diagnostics" description="What the review log says about how this material is being remembered.">
  <article class="page">
    <header class="page__header">
      <p class="page__kicker">Trainer</p>
      <h1>Diagnostics</h1>
      <p class="page__summary">
        What the review log says, computed in this browser from the history stored here. Nothing on
        this page is a score. The useful readings are the ones that tell you something is wrong:
        a deck retained far below the others, a calibration curve off the diagonal, a card that has
        been forgotten five times.
      </p>
      <p><a href={`${base}/flashcards/`}>← back to the trainer</a></p>
    </header>

    <div class="dash" data-dashboard>
      <p class="trainer__fatal" data-el="fatal" hidden></p>
      <p class="trainer__note" data-el="note"></p>

      <p class="trainer__note" data-el="empty" hidden>
        No reviews recorded yet. Do a session and this page will have something to measure.
      </p>

      <div data-el="panels">
        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>True retention</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Of the reviews of cards already in the Review state, the fraction recalled — anything
            but <em>Again</em>. Learning and relearning steps are excluded: they happen minutes
            apart and are almost always passed, and counting them is how most apps report 95%
            regardless of what the scheduler was asked for.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            Compare against the target the scheduler was configured with (0.90). Persistently below
            it means intervals are too long; well above it means you are reviewing more than you
            need to.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__big"><b data-el="retention-overall">—</b>
            <span>over <b data-el="retention-n">0</b> mature reviews</span></p>

          <table class="dash__table">
            <thead><tr><th>deck</th><th>retention</th><th>recalled</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody data-el="retention-table"></tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="trainer__note" data-el="retention-empty" hidden>
            No mature reviews yet — every card is still in its learning steps.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>Calibration</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Before each review the model predicted a recall probability from the card's stability
            and the time elapsed. Each review row stores that state, so the prediction can be
            recomputed exactly and compared with what happened. Points are grouped into deciles of
            predicted recall; dot area is the number of reviews behind the point.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            On the diagonal means the model's confidence about <em>you</em> is right. Below it means
            overconfident — the intervals it chooses are longer than your memory supports. This is
            the one panel that can say the scheduler is wrong rather than that you are.
          </p>
          <div class="dash__chart" data-el="calibration-chart"></div>
          <p class="dash__verdict" data-el="calibration-verdict"></p>
          <p class="dash__foot">
            mean absolute calibration error <b data-el="calibration-error">—</b>
            over <b data-el="calibration-n">0</b> reviews
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>Where the deck is</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Cards by FSRS state. <em>New</em> has never been seen; <em>Learning</em> is in its first
            steps; <em>Review</em> is on a real interval; <em>Relearning</em> is a card that was
            consolidated and then forgotten.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            A large Relearning share is the signal worth acting on: it usually means the cards are
            wrong, not that the memory is.
          </p>
          <div class="dash__statebar" data-el="state-bar"></div>
          <ul class="trainer__spread" data-el="state-legend"></ul>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>Memory strength by deck</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Median stability — the interval at which recall would fall to 90% — and mean recall
            probability right now. Median rather than mean, because one card on a four-year
            interval drags an average somewhere no card actually is.
          </p>
          <table class="dash__table">
            <thead><tr><th>deck</th><th>cards</th><th>median stability</th><th>recall now</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody data-el="strength-table"></tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>Leeches</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Cards forgotten three or more times after reaching the Review state. Forgetting during
            learning is learning; forgetting something repeatedly after consolidating it usually
            means the card is at fault — two facts in one, or an answer that does not follow from
            the question.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            This is a worklist. The fix is to rewrite the card in
            <code>src/content/flashcards/</code>, keeping its <code>id</code> so the history
            survives — not to see it more often.
          </p>
          <ul class="dash__leeches" data-el="leeches-list"></ul>
          <p class="trainer__note" data-el="leeches-empty" hidden>
            Nothing has been forgotten often enough to qualify.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>Activity</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Reviews per day over the last 17 weeks, oldest first. Empty days are shown as empty
            cells rather than omitted.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            There is no streak here on purpose. A run of consecutive days is a fact about a
            calendar, and protecting one is a reason to rate dishonestly at midnight.
          </p>
          <div class="dash__grid" data-el="activity-grid"></div>
          <p class="dash__foot" data-el="activity-summary"></p>
        </section>

        <section class="dash__panel">
          <h2>What is coming</h2>
          <p class="dash__what">
            Cards already scheduled for each of the next fourteen days, before any new ones are
            introduced. <b data-el="forecast-overdue">0</b> are overdue right now.
          </p>
          <p class="dash__read">
            The cost of a new card is not today, it is this chart a year from now.
          </p>
          <div class="dash__forecast" data-el="forecast-chart"></div>
          <p class="dash__foot" data-el="forecast-scale"></p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script type="application/json" data-cards set:html={JSON.stringify(cards)} />

  <script>
    import { bootDashboard } from '../../lib/dashboard.mjs';

    const root = document.querySelector('[data-dashboard]');
    const cards = JSON.parse(document.querySelector('[data-cards]').textContent);
    bootDashboard({ root, cards });
  </script>
</Base>
